 the  secretary of state had himself  intervened  the panel it says is not persuaded that  there is an arguable case that the court  of appeal has erred and accordingly  refuses permission to appeal to this  court it says that the application of  course was for the stay to be extended  so that the united nations convention um  on the rights of persons with  disabilities could consider arch's case  it says that the panel is satisfied not  only that the court of appeal has not  heard in the sense mentioned above but  that it made the correct decision that  is the decision yesterday uh to not uh  allow an extension it reaches this view  it says and then it outlines a number of  reasons and it will come as a blow to  the family who had hoped that the  supreme court might take this matter uh  further for them and that any government  involvement would look at it  in a sense of  the importance of international law the  family argue that that that convention  um is binding on uk law but clearly here  the secretary of state has intervened to  say that it isn't um we did uh  speak to the family earlier we asked  them about how they felt and they said  that um  they  were hoping that  the supreme court can give them some  encouraging signs but this will be a  blow and we wait to see what their next  steps could be with the trust we know  have said that they were waiting for the  outcome of today's decision before they  proceed with withdrawing treatment  remember that stay had expired at midday  today  after which it is now uh permissible for  the  for the trust to start withdrawing  treatment uh we again we await to see  what the trust say they did say to us  that they will be releasing a statement  shortly  and sadia not not to sort of labor the  point but just just remind us all uh  what remaining steps there are if any  for archie's family  well the supreme court is the uk's  highest appeals court so at least on a  domestic level there doesn't appear to  be anywhere further to go um the the  supreme court being the highest appeals  court for civil cases  internationally there was there were  always several options there was the  european court but also the united  nations and the family had chosen to go  to the united nations specifically  because of this  right that the united nations offers  under that convention for the rights of  persons with disabilities  and they looked specifically articles 10  and 12 articles 10 was to the right to  life and article 12 is about equality  for disabled persons now archie's family  say that it's not about the level of  disability all disability should be  treated equal to everyone else and the  fact that  doctors here are saying his prospects  are bleak they say is irrelevant because  if somebody is disabled they should be  given every chance  to survive and every medical  intervention that they require  the trust's argument is that his  prospects are so bleak actually they've  outlined in several court cases the kind  of medical intervention that is needed  to sustain life they talked about things  like um his blood pressure has to be  monitored and regulated his body  temperature they say needs to be  monitored they've even told us about the  intricacies of having to administer  artificial teardrops every four hours  and they say that every part of his  living is being done artificially and  therefore it is in his best interest  to withdraw treatment but you know we  will wait and see what the trust says  they are of course now under a court  order to withdraw that treatment and  they can do that at any point after  midday today  they've always said that they will do it  in discussion with the family  and but the family say that they will  carry on fighting they've always said  that right to the end to explore every  viable option  to give archie more time to recover  and we await to see what they will say  thanks so much uh for that update
 

